WEBVTT

00:00:00.142 --> 00:00:03.303
Hello, and a very warm welcome to Shah Spectrum.

00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:05.201
If you're new here, welcome.

00:00:05.201 --> 00:00:09.962
If you're back again, it's lovely to have you with us once more.

00:00:10.564 --> 00:00:14.082
What does it cost when the air itself becomes the problem?

00:00:14.082 --> 00:00:20.250
Smoke from Indonesia's fires has closed schools in Malaysia, and one early estimate

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:25.175
says the bill for Singapore alone could be about double what it was in 2015.

00:00:25.853 --> 00:00:26.710
Hi, I'm Mittal Shah.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:32.822
This is an A-I-generated version of my voice, and it is narrating today's story.

00:00:33.855 --> 00:00:35.788
Let's start with what is happening.

00:00:35.788 --> 00:00:39.653
Indonesia has been fighting fires since late July.

00:00:39.653 --> 00:00:46.417
An independent satellite analysis says more than one point nine million hectares had

00:00:46.417 --> 00:00:49.420
burned by the end of September.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:53.892
That is more than double the figure a month earlier, and about thirty-eight per cent

00:00:53.892 --> 00:00:58.111
more than the same period in 2019, the worst year in that group's records.

00:00:58.111 --> 00:01:01.038
A hectare is ten thousand square metres.

00:01:01.038 --> 00:01:06.436
Indonesia's government says it cannot readily confirm these numbers and is still

00:01:06.436 --> 00:01:07.036
counting.

00:01:07.491 --> 00:01:09.041
The smoke has travelled.

00:01:09.041 --> 00:01:13.823
It has reached Malaysia, Singapore, Timor-Leste and the Philippines.

00:01:13.823 --> 00:01:18.761
Singapore has had its longest run of unhealthy air since 2015, meaning a reading

00:01:18.761 --> 00:01:22.747
above one hundred on its official air quality index.

00:01:22.747 --> 00:01:28.341
By the twelfth of October, schools had closed in several Malaysian states, including

00:01:28.341 --> 00:01:29.763
Johor and Melaka.

00:01:29.763 --> 00:01:34.030
And five firefighters have died fighting fires in East Java.

00:01:34.723 --> 00:01:36.539
Why is this happening?

00:01:36.539 --> 00:01:38.072
Two things came together.

00:01:38.072 --> 00:01:38.860
First, weather:

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:43.684
a drought since June, made worse by an unusually strong El Niño.

00:01:43.684 --> 00:01:48.674
That is a warming of Pacific Ocean waters that tends to bring dry weather to

00:01:48.674 --> 00:01:49.477
Indonesia.

00:01:49.477 --> 00:01:51.416
Second, land.

00:01:51.416 --> 00:01:55.425
Some fires appear to start when land is cleared on farms and plantations, then

00:01:55.425 --> 00:01:56.025
spread.

00:01:55.651 --> 00:02:01.364
About thirty-five per cent of the burned area was swampland, and some of it overlaps

00:02:01.364 --> 00:02:02.649
with peatland.

00:02:02.649 --> 00:02:07.066
Peat is soil made of plant matter that has partly rotted over thousands of years.

00:02:07.066 --> 00:02:13.035
It is packed with carbon, and once it catches fire it can smoulder underground for

00:02:13.035 --> 00:02:13.635
weeks.

00:02:13.291 --> 00:02:15.677
That makes it very hard to put out.

00:02:16.195 --> 00:02:17.900
Now, how does smoke turn into money?

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:19.059
People fall ill and miss work.

00:02:19.059 --> 00:02:19.659
Schools shut.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.303
Visitors stay away.

00:02:21.303 --> 00:02:22.398
Businesses slow down.

00:02:22.398 --> 00:02:27.584
After the 2015 fires, the World Bank estimated the cost to Indonesia at about

00:02:27.584 --> 00:02:32.625
sixteen billion dollars, around two per cent of its economy.

00:02:32.625 --> 00:02:39.511
For Singapore, an economist at Nanyang Technological University has made an early

00:02:39.511 --> 00:02:41.151
estimate for this year:

00:02:41.151 --> 00:02:46.163
between three point two and three point nine billion Singapore dollars, against one

00:02:46.163 --> 00:02:47.436
point eight three billion in 2015.

00:02:47.436 --> 00:02:49.621
He says part of the rise is simple:

00:02:49.621 --> 00:02:53.625
the economy is bigger, so each lost day of work costs more.

00:02:53.625 --> 00:03:00.588
It is a preliminary figure, and a fuller estimate is expected in early November.

00:03:01.199 --> 00:03:01.911
So who pays?

00:03:01.911 --> 00:03:08.764
Singapore has a law that lets it fine companies whose land causes haze, up to one

00:03:08.764 --> 00:03:11.740
hundred thousand Singapore dollars a day, capped at two million.

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:15.133
It also lets anyone who suffers a loss sue.

00:03:15.133 --> 00:03:20.954
But since the law took effect in 2014, no company has been fined or sued under it.

00:03:20.954 --> 00:03:26.436
One reason is that proving what happened on land in another country needs

00:03:26.436 --> 00:03:28.186
Indonesia's cooperation.

00:03:28.186 --> 00:03:33.216
Singapore has now sent warning notices to several Indonesian companies.

00:03:33.216 --> 00:03:35.782
Malaysia has no comparable law.

00:03:36.404 --> 00:03:41.469
In Indonesia, the government says it has sent forty-seven aircraft and sixty

00:03:41.469 --> 00:03:43.740
thousand personnel to fight the fires.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:48.434
On the twentieth of October, protesters in Jakarta and other cities said that was

00:03:48.434 --> 00:03:52.969
not enough, and pointed to inadequate government action.

00:03:53.575 --> 00:03:55.678
There are limits to what we know.

00:03:55.678 --> 00:04:01.582
The burned-area figures come from one satellite analysis that the government has not

00:04:01.582 --> 00:04:02.464
confirmed.

00:04:02.464 --> 00:04:07.555
The cost numbers are early estimates, not final accounts.

00:04:07.555 --> 00:04:10.446
And nobody can say yet when the rains will come.

00:04:10.446 --> 00:04:11.075
What to watch:

00:04:11.075 --> 00:04:14.758
whether the weather turns, and the fuller cost estimate in November.

00:04:15.374 --> 00:04:16.328
To sum up:

00:04:16.328 --> 00:04:18.625
this haze is more than bad air.

00:04:18.625 --> 00:04:24.101
It is a bill that lands on schools, workplaces and hospitals across several

00:04:24.101 --> 00:04:27.768
countries, and the question of who pays it is still mostly unanswered.

00:04:28.333 --> 00:04:32.001
This is news and explanation, not investment advice.

00:04:32.999 --> 00:04:36.468
Thanks for listening, and see you in the next episode.

